id: core/relation-address
title: "Relation Address — the slash as traversal"
kind: ontology-contract
record_shape: header
status: draft
version: 0.1.0
updated: 2026-09-30
authored_by: agent-on-behalf
authored_via: "Codex, reconciling the relationship-core proposal on behalf of the maintainer"

purpose: >
  Make a slash useful as a quiet, human-facing way to enter or traverse a
  Relationship without making a route, glyph, or nesting convention carry
  semantic identity by itself.

definition:
  human: "The slash opens the relationship."
  machine: >
    A Relation Address is a directional, time-indexed View over typed relation
    steps. A slash is its visible traversal handle; the underlying
    Relationship, participation, and policy records retain their own identity.

example:
  display_path: "dave/christianity"
  means: >
    A Dave-standpoint address into a stable relationship record concerning
    Dave and the Christianity referent. The path does not state Dave's
    religious identity, beliefs, authority, or the truth of any source.
  composition: >
    dave/christianity/jesus may traverse further typed relations, such as a
    tradition's source-attributed reference to a historical person. It does
    not establish parentage unless an explicit primary_parent step says so.

direction:
  rule: >
    dave/jaime and jaime/dave may be directional Views into one shared
    Relationship. Reversing a path does not automatically reverse authority,
    obligation, disclosure, consent, causation, or truth.

open_port:
  rule: >
    An address may be intentionally open at a known anchor. Openness is a View
    state, not an assertion of empty memory, completed obligations, pure
    consciousness, or permission to mutate the canonical record.

parentage:
  rule: >
    Only an explicit primary_parent relation establishes semantic ancestry.
    Route nesting, geometry, relatedness, and display order do not.

boundaries:
  - "A Relation Address does not prove containment, identity, agreement, consent, authority, or truth."
  - "A Relation Address does not grant publication, action, or canonical mutation."
  - "A rendered page is a View; it cannot replace the typed relation records it presents."

links:
  - schemas/relation-address.v0.1.schema.json
  - schemas/relationship.v0.1.schema.json
  - core/relationship.yawn
  - core/participation.yawn
  - core/holarchy.yawn
  - spec/ontology.md

proof:
  covers: [definition, example, direction, open_port, parentage, boundaries]
  tests:
    - tests/relationship-core-v0.1.test.mjs
  fixtures:
    - fixtures/relationship-core.v0.1.json
  status: proposed-until-maintainer-review
